= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A smooth real <vector bundle> of <vector bundle rank> $r$ consists of a <smooth manifold> $E$, a smooth projection $\pi:E\to M$, real <vector space> structures on its fibers, and local <vector bundle trivializations>
$$
\Phi_U:\pi^{-1}(U)\longrightarrow U\times\mathbb R^r
$$
that commute with projection to $U$ and are <linear isomorphisms> on each fiber. On overlaps the change of trivialization is $(p,v)\mapsto(p,g_{VU}(p)v)$, with $g_{VU}:U\cap V\to GL_r(\mathbb R)$ smooth into the <general linear group>. The usual <smooth manifold> convention is <Hausdorff> and <second countable>.

For an $n$-dimensional $M$, take the disjoint union of its <tangent spaces>. A <manifold chart> $x:U\to\mathbb R^n$ supplies
$$
\Phi_x(v_p)=(p,dx_p(v_p)),\qquad
\widetilde\Phi_x(v_p)=(x(p),dx_p(v_p)).
$$
If $y$ is another chart, the resulting change of coordinates on the total space is
$$
(a,v)\longmapsto\bigl(y\circ x^{-1}(a),D(y\circ x^{-1})_a v\bigr).
$$
This is smooth, its inverse is the same construction with $x,y$ exchanged, and the fiber map is invertible and linear. Thus these charts define a smooth structure with the required bundle trivializations.

For completeness, the topology so constructed is <Hausdorff>: different base points can be separated downstairs, while vectors over one point can be separated inside one product chart. A countable base atlas for $M$ and countable bases for its products with $\mathbb R^n$ give <second countability>. Hence the total space is genuinely a <smooth manifold>, not just a collection of fibers. We obtain
$$
\boxed{TM\longrightarrow M\text{ is a smooth vector bundle of rank }n.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The fibers must retain their base labels. In particular, a literal untagged union of fibers in $E$ is not sufficient when $f$ is not <injective>. Use the <pullback vector bundle>
$$
f^*E=\{(p,e)\in N\times E:f(p)=\pi(e)\},\qquad \pi_f(p,e)=p.
$$
This makes precise the indexed-union notation in the PDF. For a <vector bundle trivialization> $\Phi_U(e)=(\pi(e),v)$, define
$$
\Psi_U:\pi_f^{-1}(f^{-1}U)\longrightarrow f^{-1}U\times\mathbb R^r,
\qquad(p,e)\longmapsto(p,v).
$$
It is a fiberwise <linear isomorphism>, with inverse $(p,v)\mapsto(p,\Phi_U^{-1}(f(p),v))$. The transition functions are $g_{VU}\circ f$, hence smooth. They satisfy the same cocycle identities as those of $E$ and give a smooth total space of <manifold dimension> $\dim N+r$.

One may also see that this is an <embedded submanifold> of $N\times E$: in a bundle chart the constraint is the graph of the smooth map $f$ in the base coordinates, leaving the $r$ fiber coordinates free. Thus the chart construction agrees with its natural subspace smooth structure. No immersion or surjectivity hypothesis on $f$ is required. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f^*E\longrightarrow N\text{ is a rank-}r\text{ vector bundle}.}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <vector bundle morphism> over $M$ is a smooth map $F:E\to E'$ with $\pi'\circ F=\pi$ whose restriction $E_p\to E'_p$ is linear for every $p$. A <vector bundle isomorphism> is such a map with a smooth bundle-morphism inverse. Equivalently, a fiberwise bijective smooth bundle morphism is an isomorphism: in local <vector bundle trivializations> it is multiplication by an invertible smooth <matrix>, and its inverse <matrix> is smooth.

For a <diffeomorphism> $f:N\to M$, define
$$
F:TN\longrightarrow f^*TM,\qquad v_p\longmapsto(p,df_p(v_p)).
$$
The derivative $df_p$ is a <linear isomorphism> by the <chain rule>, since $d(f^{-1})_{f(p)}\circ df_p=\operatorname{id}$. In <manifold charts>, $F$ is represented by the smooth <Jacobian matrix> of $f$, so it is a smooth bundle morphism. Its inverse is
$$
(p,w_{f(p)})\longmapsto d(f^{-1})_{f(p)}w_{f(p)},
$$
which is also smooth and fiberwise linear. Consequently
$$
\boxed{TN\cong f^*TM\quad\text{as vector bundles over }N.}
$$
This identifies the bundles over the same base $N$; the tangent map by itself is a map from $TN$ to $TM$ covering $f$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Under the standard <smooth manifold> hypotheses, the first answer is **yes**, although the isomorphism requires a choice. A <partition of unity> combines Euclidean <inner products> in <manifold charts> to produce a <Riemannian metric> $g$. Nonnegative local weights summing to one preserve positivity, and local finiteness preserves smoothness. The <musical isomorphism> is
$$
\boxed{\flat_g:TM\longrightarrow T^*M,\qquad v\longmapsto g(v,\cdot).}
$$
It is smooth and fiberwise invertible, with inverse having local <matrix> $(g_{ij})^{-1}$. It is therefore a <vector bundle isomorphism>. There is no preferred such map before a metric or equivalent additional structure is chosen.

The second answer is **no**. On $S^1=\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z$, compare the <trivial vector bundle> $S^1\times\mathbb R$ with the <Möbius line bundle>
$$
L=(\mathbb R\times\mathbb R)/((t+2\pi,a)\sim(t,-a)).
$$
Both have rank one. A <continuous> <section of a vector bundle> of $L$ is represented on $[0,2\pi]$ by a <continuous> function $s$ satisfying $s(2\pi)=-s(0)$. If it were nowhere zero, the <intermediate value theorem> would prevent its endpoint signs from being opposite. Thus $L$ has no <nowhere-zero section>. The trivial bundle has the section $1$, and a bundle isomorphism would carry that section to a nowhere-zero section of $L$, a contradiction. Hence
$$
\boxed{L\not\cong S^1\times\mathbb R.}
$$

